                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p26-q40-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Compare ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "a",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " with the perpendicular height and ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "c",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ". Only this strict range gives two valid positions for ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "C",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "By part (a), $h=c\\sin A$. The point $C$ must lie on the rightward baseline ray from $A$, with $BC=a$.\n\nIf $a<h$, the side cannot reach the baseline, so no triangle exists. If $a=h$, $C$ is the perpendicular foot, giving one right triangle.\n\nIf $h<a<c$, there are two baseline intersections. Since $a<BA=c$, the intersection on the $A$-side of the perpendicular foot lies between $A$ and the foot; the other lies beyond the foot. Both give valid triangles. The angle at $C_1$ is obtuse and the angle at $C_2$ is acute.\n\nIf $a=c$, one intersection is $A$ and is degenerate, leaving one triangle. If $a>c$, one intersection lies behind $A$, again leaving one triangle.\n\nThus exactly two triangles occur when $h<a<c$. Substituting $h=c\\sin A$ gives\n$$c\\sin A<a<c.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 88
      }
